A few weeks ago, I thought building a data engineering project meant creating tables, writing ETL pipelines, and loading data into a warehouse.

The deeper I went, the more I realized that real-world projects start much earlier.

Before writing a single SQL query, we started by asking:

What business are we solving for?

Instead of jumping directly into Snowflake, DBT, or dashboards, we chose a domain and began thinking like architects.

For our project, we selected a Multi-Channel Retail & Supply Chain Enterprise where customers, suppliers, warehouses, stores, inventory, orders, payments, shipments, and returns all generate business events and data.

So far, we've completed:

✅ Domain Research

Retail business processes
Industry systems (Shopify, SAP Retail, Oracle Retail, Amazon Seller)
Stakeholder analysis
Analytics use cases

✅ Business Requirements

Revenue visibility
Inventory optimization
Customer analytics
Supplier performance tracking
Executive reporting

✅ Conceptual Data Modeling (CDM)

Customer
Product
Supplier
Warehouse
Inventory
Order
Payment
Shipment
Return

Understanding how business entities interact before thinking about databases.

✅ Business Process Modeling

Order-to-Cash (O2C)
Procure-to-Pay (P2P)
Return-to-Refund (R2R)

Mapping how data is actually generated across enterprise systems.

✅ Logical Data Modeling (LDM)

Entity relationships
Cardinality
Business rules
Relationship matrices
Bridge table discussions
Reference data strategy

✅ Normalization Review

1NF
2NF
3NF
Redundancy elimination
Data integrity design decisions

One of the biggest lessons so far:

A data warehouse is only as good as the business model behind it.

Most tutorials start with tables.

Enterprise projects start with business processes.

Next, we'll move into:

🔹 Physical Data Modeling (PostgreSQL)

🔹 Source System Simulation

🔹 Data Generation & Ingestion

🔹 Snowflake Data Warehouse Design

🔹 Star Schema & Fact/Dimension Modeling

🔹 SCD Type 1 & Type 2

🔹 DBT Transformations

🔹 Data Quality Framework

🔹 Data Lineage

🔹 Bronze → Silver → Gold Architecture

The goal isn't just to build a project.

It's to understand how modern organizations design, govern, transform, and analyze data at scale.
